  "author": "",
  "attributionNote": "The Vajasaneyi Samhita is anonymous. Tradition associates its arrangement with the sage Yajnavalkya; the text itself is the liturgical corpus of the White Yajurveda school.",
  "description": "The White Yajurveda is the Vajasaneyi Samhita, the collection of formulae spoken by the Adhvaryu priest at the soma and animal sacrifices, with the verses of the Rigveda embedded in its prose. Unlike the Black Yajurveda, it separates the samhita from the Brahmana: the formulas are given here in forty books, from the new- and full-moon offerings through the Agnihotra, the soma sacrifice and the horse sacrifice to the Purusha hymn and the final expiation rites.",
  "historicalContext": "The Vajasaneyi Samhita survives in the Madhyandina and Kanva recensions; Griffith translates the Madhyandina text. His translation of 1899 was the first complete English rendering of the White Yajurveda and remains the standard public-domain one.",
  "availabilityNote": "Complete. All forty books of Griffith's translation are streamed from the Internet Sacred Text Archive at read time, one book per chapter. The volume's preface, contents and the index of Rigvedic and Atharvan verses are not part of the work.",
